# Authentication

<p class="text-muted-foreground text-sm">{formattedDate}</p>

Proving that I own an account should not require giving you my email address.

Email authentication gives a service a mail system to maintain: delivery, message templates, confirmation links, and address changes. Add passwords, and there is password recovery too. All of this work exists just to let someone register and come back.

A passkey lets them do that without a mailbox. When someone registers, their device or password manager creates a credential for the service. When they return, they use it to sign in. The service still has to handle credential management and decide how account recovery works, but sending email is no longer a prerequisite.

For the person signing up, email confirmation means switching to a mailbox, waiting for a message, and following a link. Passwords add another problem: remembering one encourages reuse; generating a unique one means relying on a password manager. A passkey already fits that second workflow, without a password to type into a phishing page.

## The truth

An email address gives a service more than a way to authenticate you. It gives it a way to reach you outside the service, and an identifier that you may use elsewhere.

That is useful for sending you spam. It is also useful for linking your activity across sites.

I have, unfortunately, built [data management platforms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_management_platform) (DMPs). An email address can be used to connect an account to advertising identifiers and audience segments. An address handed over just to register can become a way to recognise the same person elsewhere.

That does not mean every service sells your data. It means asking for an email creates an opportunity that authentication itself does not require. If a service wants to send me something, it should ask separately.

## It should be illegal

Requiring an email to register is one of the very few things I hope to see regulated. Where an address is unnecessary to provide the service, withholding access until I hand one over is coercion. I should be able to create an account without also giving the service a way to contact me outside it.

Every service that can work without my email should offer registration without it. Sending me mail should be a separate choice.

